# The credentials this project needs, what reads each one, and where the real
# values live. Copy this file to `.env` — in this directory or in
# `src/db_maintenance/`, `env.js` looks in both — and fill it in. The real
# `.env` is gitignored at any depth; this template is the only one tracked.
#
# Filling this in is NOT what configures the deployed site. Netlify holds its
# own copy of the runtime variables, set in its UI and readable nowhere else,
# and that copy is what the API actually runs on. #185 is the standing lesson:
# a variable set outside the repo kept the API on a dead Node for years while
# every file in the tree said otherwise. So the list below says, for each
# variable, which of the three consumers reads it — the maintenance scripts on
# a laptop, the deployed functions, or a person at a terminal.
#
# Never commit a filled-in copy, and never move one. `MEMO_ENV_FILE` exists so
# that code running from a copy on local disk can read the credentials where
# they already are, rather than the credentials being copied to meet the code:
#
#   MEMO_ENV_FILE=/path/to/this/.env node src/db_maintenance/scripts/audit_database.js
#
# Two of these match no registered provider format, so GitHub's push
# protection would not stop them being committed — see #301. For
# TMDB_API_KEY and TWITCH_CLIENT_SECRET, .gitignore is the only thing between
# the key and a public repository.

################################################################################
# Read by the maintenance scripts AND by the deployed API.
# Scripts read them through src/db_maintenance/env.js; the deployed copies are
# set in Netlify and are not read from this file.
################################################################################

# The Atlas connection string. The copy in this file is full-access by
# design — restore_backup.js and friends legitimately need more reach than the
# nightly job does. The copy in GitHub's secret store is scoped to readWrite on
# the `memo` database instead (#355). Every script names client.db("memo")
# explicitly, so the database in this URI decides nothing; the host does.
MONGODB_URL=

# api.themoviedb.org — films and TV metadata. Read by destructuring
# (`const { TMDB_API_KEY } = process.env`), so grepping for `process.env.TMDB`
# does not find it.
TMDB_API_KEY=

# api.igdb.com — game metadata, authenticated through Twitch. IGDB allows four
# requests a second (#34).
TWITCH_CLIENT_ID=
TWITCH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# googleapis.com/books — book metadata. Roughly 1,000 calls a day, and it does
# not fail cleanly at the cap: a refused page comes back empty, which is
# indistinguishable from one that found nothing. See CLAUDE.md's data traps.
GOOGLE_API_KEY=

################################################################################
# Read only by the deployed API. Nothing in src/db_maintenance touches these,
# so a laptop copy needs them only to run the API locally.
################################################################################

# The Auth0 tenant and the application within it. The client has no secret:
# the flow is response_type=id_token with response_mode=form_post, so it never
# reaches the token endpoint to authenticate at.
AUTH0_DOMAIN=
AUTH0_CLIENT_ID=

# The namespace the roles claim is filed under on the id_token, as
# `${AUTH0_TOKEN_NAMESPACE}/roles`. It must match the Auth0 action that adds
# the claim, and a mismatch reads as a user with no roles rather than an error.
AUTH0_TOKEN_NAMESPACE=

# The HS256 key that signs `nf_jwt`, which is the whole session for fourteen
# days. This is the one value here that is not a third party's — losing it
# means anyone can mint a session for any user.
TOKEN_SECRET=

################################################################################
# Set for you, or optional. Listed so that nothing on this list is a surprise.
################################################################################

# Netlify sets URL on every build and every function invocation; the auth
# handlers build the callback and logout urls out of it. Set it by hand only
# when running the functions outside Netlify.
# URL=https://nil.moe

# The username the build treats as this deployment's owner — it is what the
# sitemap and the <link rel="alternate"> tags name. Defaults to "nil".
# MEMO_OWNER=nil

# Set by the npm scripts (`dev` and `build`), not by hand. Anything other than
# "dev" is treated as production, so the fallback is the safe one.
# ELEVENTY_ENV=prod

################################################################################
# Read by no code at all — a credential for a person at a terminal.
################################################################################

# A Netlify personal access token, from User settings > Applications >
# Personal access tokens. Nothing in this repository reads it; it is for
# querying the Netlify API by hand, which is the only way to see the parts of
# this system that are not in the repo — the site's own environment variables,
# the deploy contexts, and whether anything is overriding the functions runtime
# (#185 again). Netlify PATs have no read-only scope: a token is full account
# access, including deleting the site. Create one when you need it and revoke
# it when you are done rather than leaving it here.
NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN=
